# Deliberately pinned @anthropic-ai/claude-code version for CI's provider
# installs. This is NOT "pin to hold green" — this project's doctrine is to
# never pin to an older upstream just to keep CI quiet. 2.1.257 broke
# `clode build`'s SCC merger (QuickJS's parser rejects a merged file with
# "invalid property name") on every leg that stages a provider; only
# engine-only cross-build legs still pass. This pin is the OTHER half of the
# doctrine — "absorb the change AND make the next absorption cheaper" — paid
# first: 2.1.257 gets absorbed deliberately, later, as the first test of the
# rejiggered build system, not silently avoided by holding still forever.
#
# upstream-drift.yml does NOT read this file: its whole job is noticing that
# upstream moved, so it stays on `next`/unpinned on purpose. See its own
# comment before "fixing" that for consistency with everything else here.
#
# MEASURED EVIDENCE for the pin, so the gap is a dated fact and not a memory
# (every line here is something someone observed, not inferred):
#
#   2.1.251  the pin. Carves, merges, builds, ships. Exercised on every push.
#   2.1.257  `clode build` DIES in the SCC merge — QuickJS rejects the merged file
#            with "invalid property name". Every leg that stages a provider went red;
#            only engine-only cross-build legs passed. This is why the pin exists.
#   2.1.261  independently observed uncarvable by our extractor, after native Claude
#            Code auto-updated past the pin mid-session (2026-09-04).
#   2.1.270  anchors and CLI extraction verified OK against the real darwin-arm64
#            bundle on 2026-09-12 (the Remote Control gate anchor was re-pinned that
#            day for its new object-returning shape). The MERGE is UNTESTED here: it
#            needs an engine, and nothing in CI currently attempts it against newer
#            than the pin. Filed in BACKLOG.md as an engine-backed carve probe.
#
#   2.1.278  CARVES, MERGES AND COMPILES (2026-09-21, darwin-arm64, real engine): staging
#            produced 4 merged groups (8, 4, 61, 4) and all 1958 staged modules compiled
#            under tjs. Not luck we control — see the root cause below. Nothing is claimed
#            here about booting it; only that the step the pin exists for now passes.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY BREAKS, measured 2026-09-21 on this box with a real engine, because
# "QuickJS rejects the merged file" was true and told nobody where to look. The merged
# file is not valid ES in ANY engine — node rejects it too ("Invalid or unexpected token")
# — so this was never a QuickJS parser gap. scc-merge.cjs renames colliding top-level
# bindings over a mask that calls these bytes ordinary code, and on 2.1.257 upstream's
# re-chunking put a module carrying BOTH of these into a strongly connected group for the
# first time (group 1 grew 99 -> 114 members):
#
#   \u{b5}s:1000n        ->  \__m28_u{b5}s:1000n        (a unicode escape in an IDENTIFIER;
#                                                        the `u` was renamed inside it)
#   static get rules(){  ->  static __m28_get rules(){   (a getter's member modifier)
#
# Both live in ONE carved module, chunk-1kg58a1a.js, which also declares a genuine
# top-level `function get(...)` and a top-level `u`. The first is what QuickJS reaches
# first, and it is exactly "invalid property name"; the second is caught at merge time
# today by assertNoRenamedFixedNames, which is why a 2.1.257 carve now refuses with the
# ratchet's message instead of the one recorded above. On 2.1.251 and 2.1.278 those two
# sites sit in modules that are NOT in any merged group, so the merger never touches them
# — the defects are LATENT, not fixed, and upstream's next re-chunk can re-trigger them.
#
# So: the versions between .252 and .269 have never been tried, and "we cannot build
# from newer" is measured at .257 and .261 — not at .270, and NOT at .278.
#
# THE STANDING ANSWER, kept honest by scripts/carve-probe.mjs (upstream-drift.yml's
# `carve` job). This is the outcome someone MEASURED for newer-than-pin upstream; the
# probe compares each day's real carve+merge+compile against it and goes red on either
# change — it started working, or it fails somewhere/something new. The version is
# context for a reader; the OUTCOME is what is compared. Grammar:
#     carve-probe <version> carves
#     carve-probe <version> blocked <stage> <reason substring>
carve-probe 2.1.278 carves
